        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          How to Use the Calorie Calculator
        </h2>
        <p>
          Follow these simple steps to estimate your daily energy needs, maintenance calories, and target nutrition intake:
        </p>
        <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <li><strong>Enter your age:</strong> Input your chronological age in years.</li>
          <li><strong>Select biological sex:</strong> Choose Male or Female to apply the corresponding metabolic baseline formula.</li>
          <li><strong>Enter your height and weight:</strong> Provide your height in centimeters or feet/inches, and body weight in kilograms or pounds.</li>
          <li><strong>Choose your activity level:</strong> Select the option that closest describes your weekly exercise routine and daily physical movement.</li>
          <li><strong>Select the calculation formula:</strong> Choose between the standard <em>Mifflin-St Jeor</em> equation or the <em>Revised Harris-Benedict</em> equation.</li>
          <li><strong>Choose your goal:</strong> Select whether you want to maintain your current body weight, enter a calorie deficit to lose weight, or enter a surplus to gain mass.</li>
          <li><strong>Click Calculate Calories:</strong> Review your estimated BMR, maintenance TDEE, goal target, and macro distribution.</li>
          <li><strong>Review your estimated maintenance calories and daily calorie target:</strong> Compare your daily goals against weekly totals.</li>
          <li><strong>Adjust your inputs over time:</strong> As your body weight, body composition, or physical routine shifts, recalculate to maintain accurate targets.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <!-- 2. What Are Maintenance Calories? -->
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          What Are Maintenance Calories?
        </h2>
        <p>
          Maintenance calories—frequently referred to as Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)—represent an estimate of the total number of calories your body burns in a 24-hour period. Consuming approximately this energy amount helps maintain your current body weight under consistent physical conditions.
        </p>
        <p>
          Using a dedicated <strong>maintenance calorie calculator</strong> takes into account the four primary components of human energy expenditure:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <li><strong>Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR):</strong> The energy expended at rest to maintain vital involuntary cellular, respiratory, and organ functions (~60–70% of total burn).</li>
          <li><strong>Thermic Effect of Food (TEF):</strong> The calories burned digesting, absorbing, and metabolizing nutrients (~8–10% of intake).</li>
          <li><strong>Non-Exercise Activity Thermogenesis (NEAT):</strong> Energy used for spontaneous everyday movement such as walking, fidgeting, posture maintenance, and household tasks (~15–20%).</li>
          <li><strong>Exercise Activity Thermogenesis (EAT):</strong> Calories burned during planned structured physical exercise and sports training.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- 3. How Many Calories Should I Eat to Lose Weight? -->
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          How Many Calories Should I Eat to Lose Weight?
        </h2>
        <p>
          When using a <strong>calorie calculator to lose weight</strong>, a target calorie deficit is calculated by subtracting a structured energy amount from your baseline maintenance calories.
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 text-xs sm:text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 space-y-2">
          <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Standard Calorie Deficit Guidelines:</p>
          <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
            <li><strong>Gradual Deficit (-250 kcal/day):</strong> Results in an estimated ~0.25 kg (0.55 lb) fat loss per week. Recommended for leaner individuals or those prioritizing muscle retention and high athletic performance.</li>
            <li><strong>Moderate Deficit (-500 kcal/day):</strong> Results in an estimated ~0.50 kg (1.10 lb) fat loss per week (~3,500 kcal deficit per week). Widely recognized as a balanced, sustainable pace for most adults.</li>
            <li><strong>Faster Deficit (-750 kcal/day):</strong> Results in an estimated ~0.75 kg (1.65 lb) fat loss per week. Should be monitored carefully to ensure adequate micronutrient and protein intake.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">
          <em>Note: Caloric targets should rarely fall below 1,200 kcal/day for women or 1,500 kcal/day for men without direct medical supervision, to prevent nutrient deficiencies and excessive loss of lean muscle tissue.</em>
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 4. Daily Calorie Calculation Example -->
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          Daily Calorie Calculation Example
        </h2>
        <p>
          Consider a 30-year-old male with a height of 170 cm, body weight of 70 kg, and a moderately active routine (exercise 3–5 days per week):
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 text-xs sm:text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 space-y-1.5 font-mono">
          <div>• <strong>Mifflin-St Jeor BMR:</strong> (10 × 70) + (6.25 × 170) − (5 × 30) + 5 = 700 + 1062.5 − 150 + 5 = <strong>1,630 kcal/day</strong></div>
          <div>• <strong>Activity Multiplier (Moderate):</strong> 1.55×</div>
          <div>• <strong>Estimated Maintenance TDEE:</strong> 1,630 × 1.55 = <strong class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">2,527 kcal/day</strong> (17,689 kcal/week)</div>
          <div>• <strong>Maintain Weight Target:</strong> <strong>2,527 kcal/day</strong></div>
          <div>• <strong>Gradual Weight Loss (-250 kcal):</strong> 2,527 − 250 = <strong>2,277 kcal/day</strong></div>
          <div>• <strong>Moderate Weight Loss (-500 kcal):</strong> 2,527 − 500 = <strong>2,027 kcal/day</strong></div>
          <div>• <strong>Gradual Weight Gain (+250 kcal):</strong> 2,527 + 250 = <strong>2,777 kcal/day</strong></div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 italic">
          This example is for illustration only. Individual metabolic efficiency, genetics, and thermogenesis vary.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 5. Common Mistakes -->
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          Common Mistakes When Tracking Calories
        </h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Choosing the Wrong Activity Level</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Selecting 'Very Active' for a desk job with 45 minutes of lifting overestimates daily TDEE by 300 to 500 kcal.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Forgetting Drinks and Cooking Oils</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Cooking oils, salad dressings, coffee creamers, and casual bites can inadvertently add 300+ unaccounted calories daily.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Ignoring Portion Sizes</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Eyeballing volumetric tablespoons or cups often results in 20% to 40% underreporting compared to weighing in grams.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Expecting Instant Results</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Calorie counts are mathematical models. Weekly scale weight trends over 3 to 4 weeks are the ultimate arbiter of energy balance.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 6. Important Information and Disclaimer -->
